{
  "assessment": [
    {
      "Protected expressive activity and existence of an interference": "The applicants were participating in a notified public protest against the use of animals in circus acts, holding banners and chanting slogans near the Lviv Circus; the entrance remained unobstructed and the event began peacefully (see paragraphs 5-6). Such protest activity is at the core of political and civic expression protected by Article 10, especially where the message concerns a matter of public interest. The searches, handcuffing of some applicants, removal from the protest site, transport to the police station for two to three hours, and administrative-offence charges against several applicants clearly amounted to an interference with their freedom of expression (see paragraphs 12-15). This approach is consistent with the Court’s case-law on protest-related expression and police intervention (see nos. 4048/09, 29580/12 and 31349/20)."
    },
    {
      "Legal basis and legitimate aim": "The impugned measures had a domestic-law basis in the National Police Act provisions on preventive 'surface inspection' and in the Code of Administrative Offences provisions on escorting persons to a police station and administrative arrest (see paragraphs 26-30). The authorities could also rely on legitimate aims under Article 10 § 2, namely the prevention of disorder or crime and the protection of the rights and safety of others. The tense context is important: an opposing group had arrived, one person threw a snowball, a smoke grenade was lit, and the police were facing a risk of clashes between rival groups (see paragraphs 7-10). In principle, Article 10 does not prevent the police from taking reasonable safety measures in such circumstances (see nos. 1162/22 and 31349/20)."
    },
    {
      "Weight of the security concerns": "The police concerns were not purely hypothetical. Before the broader intervention, officers found on the second applicant a metal rod concealed in his sleeve and a knife (see paragraph 8). According to the police and later findings, dangerous items were also found on a number of other demonstrators or thrown to the ground, including knives, metal rods, hammers and gas canisters (see paragraphs 11-12 and 20). Those facts would likely justify heightened vigilance and at least targeted preventive checks of persons reasonably suspected of carrying dangerous objects. The Court generally accepts that the State enjoys a certain margin of appreciation where there is credible evidence of an imminent risk of violence at a protest (see nos. 29580/12 and 31349/20)."
    },
    {
      "Lack of individualized assessment in the wider intervention": "However, the decisive issue is proportionality. After discovering prohibited or dangerous items on certain persons, the police surrounded the demonstrators and pulled them out one by one for searches, and all the applicants were then taken to the police station (see paragraphs 10, 12 and 14). The materials indicate a broad operation against the protest group as such, rather than carefully differentiated action aimed only at those in respect of whom there was individualized suspicion or probable cause. The internal investigation itself accepted that detention paperwork required by domestic law had not been drawn up, showing an insufficiently individualized and legally disciplined response (see paragraph 21). In protest cases, the Court places particular emphasis on distinguishing peaceful participants from wrongdoers and on avoiding blanket measures with a chilling effect on lawful expression (see nos. 4048/09, 1162/22 and 31349/20)."
    },
    {
      "Temporary detention and disruption of the protest as a chilling measure": "The applicants were kept at the police station for approximately two to three hours, some were handcuffed, and they were photographed and fingerprinted; yet no administrative-detention reports were drawn up, and only three applicants were formally charged with minor administrative offences (see paragraphs 13-15, 21 and 23). This suggests that, for many of them, the main practical effect of the police action was to terminate or seriously disrupt their participation in the demonstration rather than to pursue concrete proceedings based on individual misconduct. Even a short deprivation of liberty in the context of a public protest can create a strong chilling effect on future participation in expressive events, particularly where procedural safeguards are not respected (see nos. 4048/09 and 29580/12)."
    },
    {
      "Offensive slogans and isolated misconduct": "The slogans reported in the video included 'Antifa', 'Nazis, police, the same coalition', and at least one insulting expletive aimed at the opposing group (see paragraph 9). One applicant was later charged with obscene language and not responding to police remarks (see paragraph 15). Still, Article 10 protects expression that may offend, shock or disturb, and isolated vulgarity or hostility does not by itself justify dispersing or removing an entire protest group. Nor do the facts show that the applicants as a whole engaged in actual violence. The existence of some dangerous items justified targeted intervention, but not the wholesale suppression of the protest-related expression of all applicants without a more exacting individualized assessment (see nos. 29580/12 and 31349/20)."
    },
    {
      "Overall conclusion under Article 10": "In sum, while the authorities had genuine public-safety concerns and were entitled to conduct focused searches of individuals reasonably suspected of carrying dangerous objects, the subsequent collective removal of all applicants from the demonstration, their transport to the police station for hours, and the absence of proper individualized detention records point to a disproportionate response to a protest that had begun peacefully and concerned a matter of public interest (see paragraphs 5-6, 10-15 and 21). The interference therefore appears not to have been 'necessary in a democratic society' within the meaning of Article 10 § 2. The most likely outcome is a finding of a violation of Article 10 (see nos. 4048/09, 1162/22, 29580/12 and 31349/20)."
    }
  ],
  "violation": "yes"
}
